/-! # Smoothening a model with smooth generic fibre

Source: Bosch--Lütkebohmert--Raynaud, Néron Models, §3.1, Theorem 3 and Corollary 4,
printed pp. 61--62 (blueprint: lem-sfb-generic-smooth-model-smoothening).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

/-- A separated finite-type model with smooth generic fibre has a smooth separated
finite-type model mapping to it, with the same generic fibre and the same points over
the strict henselization at the chosen geometric closed point. Points are morphisms
in the category over `Spec R`, and their map is composition with `q`.

Source: Bosch--Lütkebohmert--Raynaud, Néron Models, §3.1, Corollary 4, obtained from
Theorem 3 by restricting to the smooth locus. The blowups preserve separatedness.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-generic-smooth-model-smoothening).

Proof:
1. Put A = R^sh. The affine strict-local-ring comparison identifies this geometric
construction with the algebraic strict henselization. The strict-henselization DVR
and maximal-ideal lemmas show that A is a flat DVR with maximal ideal generated by
any uniformizer π of R. Replace each affine algebra B of Y by the image of
B → B[1/π]. Its kernel is the π-power torsion, which commutes with localization;
these quotients glue to a closed model X. Every A-point kills this kernel, and
localizing at π kills it too, so X(A) = Y(A) and X_E = Y_E. Its affine algebras are
torsion free over the PID R, hence flat by the PID torsion-free flatness lemma.
The quotient construction preserves finite type and separatedness.
2. For a flat model V, the section differential-defect criterion identifies the
length of the torsion in a section's pulled-back differentials with a finite
nonnegative defect, zero exactly when its closed image is smooth. An open of
Spec A containing its closed point is all of Spec A, so defect zero means the
whole section factors through the smooth locus. The uniform section-differential
bound bounds these defects. Induct on a bound c for any chosen subset of sections,
requiring centres to lie in the nonsmooth locus over the closure of its reductions.
For c = 0, including the empty subset, no modification is needed.
3. Discard sections already smooth. Finite type over R makes V Noetherian;
relative differentials are of finite type and thus coherent here. Apply the
separable-point cotangent-filtration lemma to their special-fibre restriction
and to the reductions of the remaining sections. It gives a finite partition
E_1,...,E_t with nested closed strata Z_1,...,Z_t and dense smooth opens U_i on
which the differential module is locally free. These strata lie in the nonsmooth
locus. Induct also on t, with the empty partition requiring no modification.
4. Blow up Z_t. The trait blowup section-charts lemma preserves flatness,
separatedness, finite type, the generic fibre, and all sections uniquely. Lifted
sections of E_t lie in the dilatation chart. Density in the filtration implies
condition (N), the vanishing of the intersection of evaluation kernels. Together
with smoothness of U_t and local freeness there, the permissible-dilatation
estimate lowers each defect in E_t to at most max(0,c-1). The chart is open and
contains the whole lifted section, so this is also its defect in the full blowup.
5. All other sections reduce outside Z_t, where the blowup is an isomorphism,
so their defects are unchanged. Apply the outer induction at c-1 to E_t. The
support requirement keeps its later centres over Z_t, and hence away from the
other sections. Sections made smooth remain smooth because all centres lie in
the nonsmooth locus.
6. Recompute the canonical filtration for the remaining sections. On the unchanged
open outside Z_t, reduced closure, smooth loci and local-freeness loci restrict
to those of the old filtration. Thus it has at most the old t-1 remaining parts;
extra exceptional closure points do not create a part with a remaining section.
Their defects are still bounded by c. The inner induction resolves them. This
completes both inductions and preserves the support condition on the centres.
7. Apply the construction to all sections and their uniform defect bound to get
W → X → Y, unchanged on generic fibres and bijective on A-points. Take the union
of the smooth opens of W as Ysm. Smoothness is local on the source. The defect
criterion and the local-spectrum argument make every A-section factor through
Ysm, and its open immersion is a monomorphism, giving the required bijection.
8. The generic fibre is smooth and is an open of W because Spec E = D(π) in
Spec R; the smooth-open restriction lemma makes this a smooth R-open, contained
in Ysm. Hence Ysm has the whole original generic fibre. W is Noetherian and its
open Ysm is quasi-compact. A finite refinement by principal affine opens has
finite-type coordinate algebras (adjoin the inverse of the principal element),
so Ysm is of finite type. Its diagonal is the restriction of W's closed diagonal,
so it is separated. The composite Ysm → W → X → Y is the asserted map. -/
theorem exists_smoothening_of_smooth_generic_fiber
    (R E : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [IsDiscreteValuationRing R]
    [Field E] [Algebra R E] [IsFractionRing R E]
    (Ω : Type u) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
    (s : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ Spec (.of R))
    (hs : ∀ x : Spec (.of Ω), (s.base x).asIdeal = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    (Y : Over (Spec (.of R))) [IsSeparated Y.hom]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType Y.hom] [QuasiCompact Y.hom]
    [Smooth (pullback.snd Y.hom (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R E))))] :
    ∃ (Ysm : Over (Spec (.of R))) (q : Ysm ⟶ Y),
      Smooth Ysm.hom ∧ IsSeparated Ysm.hom ∧
      LocallyOfFiniteType Ysm.hom ∧ QuasiCompact Ysm.hom ∧
      IsIso (((Over.pullback (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R E)))).map q).left) ∧
      Function.Bijective
        (fun a : Over.mk (strictHenselizationStructuralMap s) ⟶ Ysm ↦ a ≫ q) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
